  "response": "The Tasaday were publicized in 1971 as a forest-dwelling group in South Cotabato province on the island of Mindanao. Their celebrity was the work of Manuel Elizalde Jr., who headed PANAMIN, the Philippine government agency charged with assistance to national minorities, and it was his command of that agency that brought the group to the attention of the world's press. Such access as the publicity had opened was not to last. The Tasaday area was closed to outside visitors in 1976, and that closure cannot be squared with the visit that Swiss journalist Oswald Iten paid to the Tasaday in 1986, shortly after Ferdinand Marcos was driven from power.\n\nThe dispute itself turned on descriptions of how these people actually lived. Elizalde's position at the head of PANAMIN gave him control of the publicity, and it produced the image of the group that the world received. Elizalde presented the Tasaday to the world as cave dwellers who used stone tools and practiced no agriculture. Iten reported finding Tasaday people living in wooden houses and wearing manufactured clothing. What he found led him to publish his allegation of a staged hoax in the Swiss newspaper Neue Z\u00fcrcher Zeitung, and that article in turn prompted the National Geographic Society formally to retract its 1972 Tasaday article in 1987.\n\nThe adjudication that counted, however, came not from journalism but from comparative linguistics, which could address directly the question of how long the group had lived apart from its neighbours. The linguist Lawrence Reid estimated that the Tasaday had separated from their Manobo-speaking relatives roughly 150 years before their 1971 publicization; the fieldwork in which he showed Tasaday speech to be closely related to Cotabato Manobo confirms that dating. A divergence of that depth placed the Tasaday within the recent history of the Cotabato Manobo world rather than in Palaeolithic isolation.\n\nOther investigators pursued the question by other means. Historian Zeus Salazar argued that the Tasaday were a fabrication staged for the political benefit of the Marcos government. Genealogical research by Emmanuel Nabayra and Amelia Rogel-Rara traced kinship ties between the Tasaday and neighboring communities. The kinship links so traced confirm the shallow time depth that Reid's estimate had assigned to the group's separation. Radiocarbon dating of charcoal from the Tasaday caves established continuous human occupation for 3,000 years. The recency of the split itself indicates the finding of the 1992 scholarly volume assessing the evidence, which determined that the Tasaday were neither a Stone Age survival nor a hoax\u2014a verdict that cannot be squared with three millennia of unbroken habitation in those same caves.",
